et move that flips one piece (A6) and avoids the immediate tactical blunders associated with the other three options. Moves B8 and C8 allow the opponent to wedge in and secure the edge, while C7 gives the opponent a massive flip from C8. A7 preserves the tension and sets up a better endgame sequence.\n\nReason: Playing A7 avoids giving the opponent easy access to the edge squares (B8 and C8) which would allow them to consolidate their position next to the corner A8. It forces the opponent to find a move elsewhere (likely C7), which you can then counter effectively.\n\nAnswer: (A, 7)'}
